# Security Rules - Rank Math API Manager Plugin

## 🛡️ Core Security Principles

### 1. Input Validation & Sanitization

- **ALWAYS validate and sanitize ALL user input**
- Use WordPress sanitization functions:

  ```php
  // Text fields
  $title = sanitize_text_field($_POST['title']);

  // Textarea content
  $description = sanitize_textarea_field($_POST['description']);

  // URLs
  $canonical_url = esc_url_raw($_POST['canonical_url']);

  // HTML content (with allowed tags)
  $content = wp_kses_post($_POST['content']);

  // Numeric values
  $post_id = intval($_POST['post_id']);
  ```

### 2. Output Escaping

- **NEVER trust data from the database or user input**
- Always escape output:

  ```php
  // HTML output
  echo esc_html($user_data);

  // HTML attributes
  echo '<input value="' . esc_attr($value) . '">';

  // URLs
  echo '<a href="' . esc_url($url) . '">Link</a>';

  // JavaScript
  echo '<script>var data = "' . esc_js($data) . '";</script>';
  ```

### 3. Nonce Verification

- **ALWAYS implement nonce verification for forms and AJAX**
- Create nonces:
  ```php
  wp_nonce_field('rank_math_api_action', 'rank_math_api_nonce');
  ```
- Verify nonces:
  ```php
  if (!wp_verify_nonce($_POST['rank_math_api_nonce'], 'rank_math_api_action')) {
      wp_die('Security check failed');
  }
  ```

### 4. Capability Checks

- **ALWAYS check user capabilities before performing actions**
- Use WordPress capability system:
  ```php
  if (!current_user_can('edit_posts')) {
      return new WP_Error('insufficient_permissions', 'User cannot edit posts');
  }
  ```

## 🔐 API Security

### 1. Authentication

- Implement WordPress Application Passwords
- Use Basic Authentication with proper encoding
- Validate user credentials on every request
- Implement proper session management

### 2. Authorization

- Check user capabilities for each operation
- Validate post ownership when applicable
- Implement role-based access control
- Use WordPress user roles and capabilities

### 3. Rate Limiting

- Implement request rate limiting
- Track API usage per user/IP
- Set reasonable limits for API endpoints
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes

### 4. Input Validation for API

- Validate all API parameters
- Check data types and formats
- Implement proper error responses
- Sanitize all input data

## 🚫 Security Anti-Patterns

### What NOT to Do

- ❌ Never use `eval()` or `exec()`
- ❌ Never trust `$_GET`, `$_POST`, or `$_REQUEST` without sanitization
- ❌ Never output user data without escaping
- ❌ Never store sensitive data in plain text
- ❌ Never use weak passwords or authentication
- ❌ Never expose internal system information in error messages

### What TO Do

- ✅ Always validate and sanitize input
- ✅ Always escape output
- ✅ Always use WordPress security functions
- ✅ Always implement proper error handling
- ✅ Always log security events
- ✅ Always use HTTPS in production

## 🔍 Security Testing

### 1. Input Validation Testing

- Test with malicious input (XSS, SQL injection attempts)
- Test with oversized data
- Test with special characters
- Test with null/empty values

### 2. Authentication Testing

- Test with invalid credentials
- Test with expired sessions
- Test with insufficient permissions
- Test with malformed authentication headers

### 3. API Security Testing

- Test rate limiting
- Test input validation
- Test error handling
- Test authentication bypass attempts

## 📋 Security Checklist

### Before Every Commit

- [ ] All user input is validated and sanitized
- [ ] All output is properly escaped
- [ ] Nonce verification is implemented
- [ ] Capability checks are in place
- [ ] No sensitive data is logged
- [ ] Error messages don't expose system information
- [ ] SQL queries use prepared statements
- [ ] File uploads are validated
- [ ] HTTPS is enforced in production
- [ ] Rate limiting is implemented

### Before Every Release

- [ ] Security audit completed
- [ ] All known vulnerabilities are patched
- [ ] Security testing passed
- [ ] Documentation updated with security notes
- [ ] Security policy is current
- [ ] Dependencies are up to date

## 🚨 Security Incident Response

### 1. Immediate Actions

- Assess the scope of the incident
- Secure affected systems
- Preserve evidence
- Notify stakeholders

### 2. Investigation

- Identify the root cause
- Document the incident
- Assess impact
- Plan remediation

### 3. Remediation

- Fix the vulnerability
- Test the fix
- Deploy the fix
- Monitor for recurrence

### 4. Post-Incident

- Update security procedures
- Conduct lessons learned
- Update documentation
- Improve monitoring

